        "description": "<figure>\n\n<img alt=\"\" data-caption=\"\" data-portal-copyright=\"\" data-has-syndication-rights=\"1\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/VRG_VRH_Chromebook_Site.jpg?quality=90&#038;strip=all&#038;crop=0,0,100,100\" />\n\t<figcaption>\n\t\t</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Google didn't even call the first Chromebook a \"Chromebook.\" The Cr-48 had no interesting design and no bright logos - it wasn't even designed to be a product you could buy. Instead, it was a portal to a big new idea about computers. </p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In this week's episode of <a href=\"https://pod.link/1840983742\"><em>Version History,</em></a> <em>The Verge</em>'s Nilay Patel and MKBHD's Marques Brownlee join David Pierce to help explain the history of ChromeOS and Chromebooks, and discuss whether Google's approach to computer hardware was the right one.</p>\n<div class=\"megaphone-embed\"><a href=\"https://playlist.megaphone.fm/?e=VMP5692627763\" target=\"_blank\" ",
